Short answer: it depends on the country. Two common rules apply:

- “Six‑month validity” rule (most common): your passport must be valid for at least 6 months after your planned date of departure. If you want a six‑month visa and will be in the country for the full six months, that usually means your passport should be valid for about 12 months from your entry date (6 months of stay + 6 months of required post‑departure validity).
- “Valid for duration of stay” rule (less strict): the passport only needs to be valid for the entire period you’ll be in the country (i.e., through the visa end date). Some countries and some visa types follow this.

